@node CMacs Deixis
@chapter CMacs Deixis: ``move THAT over THERE''

Deictic voice: you say ``move that over there'', ``summarise this'',
``close that'' while pointing --- with the mouse, a VR laser or a
tracked hand --- and each demonstrative is bound to what was being
pointed at @emph{at the moment the word was spoken}.  Not at the moment
the transcript arrives: by then, a second or two later, the pointer has
moved on to the second thing, which is why ``move that there'' cannot be
done by asking where the pointer is now.

It is a fusion layer over three things that already existed:

@table @asis
@item Words with times
The recognizers (sherpa-onnx, whisper), through
@code{cmacs-deixis-result-words}: each word's offset inside the
utterance's audio.
@item A clock
The voice loop's @code{cmacs-voice-loop-segment-start-functions}: when
that audio began, on the pointer history's clock.
@item Where the pointer was
@file{cmacs/gowl/cmacs-gowl-pointer-history.c}, or another registered
pointer: what was under the pointer at a past time.
@end table

Turn it on with @kbd{M-x cmacs-deixis-mode} (a global minor mode)
alongside @code{cmacs-voice-loop-mode}.  There is no configure flag: the
Lisp is always there, and the compositor's ring is part of
@option{--with-cmacs-gowl}.

@menu
* Deixis Utterances::       What happens to what you say.
* Deixis Word Times::       Where each word's time comes from.
* Deixis Clock::            The clock, and how wrong it can be.
* Deixis Pointers::         The gowl pointer history and other devices.
* Deixis Commands::         Commands that run with no model.
* Deixis Privacy::          What is kept, and for how long.
* Deixis Customization::    The variables.
@end menu

@node Deixis Utterances
@section What happens to an utterance

With the mode on, the voice loop hands every final utterance to
@code{cmacs-deixis-filter} before its route
(@code{cmacs-voice-loop-utterance-filter-function}).  The filter takes
the recognizer's words and their times, finds the demonstratives
(@code{cmacs-deixis-find}), binds each to what the pointer rested on
while it was spoken (@code{cmacs-deixis-resolve}), runs a local command
if the utterance is one (@code{cmacs-deixis-run-command} --- no model
involved, nothing sent anywhere), otherwise offers it to
@code{cmacs-deixis-unclaimed-functions} (called with @code{(TEXT
BINDINGS)} until one returns non-@code{nil}, which means it dealt with
the utterance --- this is where deictic @emph{questions} are recognised,
@pxref{CMacs Deixis Ask}; being asked only after the commands, a
function there can never take an utterance a command matches), and
otherwise sends the utterance on
to the loop's route @emph{annotated} (@code{cmacs-deixis-annotate}).  An
utterance with no demonstrative passes through untouched.  A pending
yes/no confirmation is answered before the filter is asked.

@example
move that [that=①] over there [there=②]

[Pointing: what each marked word referred to when it was spoken]
① window: librewolf — 'wlroots docs' (client 12)
② monitor 1 (DP-2) at 2900,400
@end example

The legend names things by id --- client id, buffer name and position,
monitor index --- so a tool-capable model can act on ``that'' with the
tools it already has.  Like @code{cmacs-ai-view} it is a pointer and
never the thing: window titles and buffer names, never buffer text.  A
demonstrative with nothing pointed at is marked @samp{[that=?]}, and a
referent looked up against the screen as it is now is marked
@samp{[approximate]}.  On the @code{commands} voice route the utterance
is left plain; the bindings are in @code{cmacs-deixis-last-bindings}
(see @code{cmacs-deixis-annotate-for-commands}).

@code{cmacs-deixis-resolve} returns one plist per demonstrative, in
spoken order: @code{:word}, @code{:token}, @code{:index}, @code{:role},
@code{:time} (ms, or @code{nil}), @code{:approx}, @code{:referent} and
@code{:marker} (words bound to the same thing share one).  A referent is
a plist with @code{:kind} (@code{window}, @code{buffer-position},
@code{region}, @code{point}, @code{file}, @code{url}, @code{monitor},
@code{tag} or @code{place}), @code{:label}, @code{:confidence},
@code{:device}, and the fields of its kind: @code{:client}
@code{:client-id} @code{:app-id} @code{:title}; @code{:buffer}
@code{:window} @code{:position}; @code{:beg} @code{:end};
@code{:monitor} @code{:x} @code{:y}; @code{:path}; @code{:url};
@code{:tag}.  @code{:stale} marks a window that closed between the word
and the lookup (it has no @code{:client}); @code{:approximate} one
resolved against the screen as it is now.

The rules:

@itemize
@item
@emph{Explicit state beats pointing.}  With the region active, an
@code{object} word (``this'', ``that'') or ``it'' means the region ---
unless the pointer clearly rested on a different window (confidence at
least @code{cmacs-deixis-elsewhere-confidence}, default 0.8).  A
@code{place} word is never the region.  The region is tested when the
transcript arrives, not when the word was spoken.
@item
@emph{The longest rest wins.}  People point a little before or after the
word, so the pointer is asked about a window from
@code{cmacs-deixis-window-before-ms} (150) before the word starts to
@code{cmacs-deixis-window-after-ms} (300) after it ends, and the answer
is where it dwelt longest there.
@item
@emph{``It''} is bound only when it can refer to something: to what was
pointed at while it was said, else to the @code{object} word before it
in the same utterance.  ``It is raining'' is never a demonstrative
(@code{cmacs-deixis-pronoun-blockers}).
@item
@emph{``That'' as a conjunction} (``I think that we should'') is skipped
by looking at the next word (@code{cmacs-deixis-clause-followers}).
This is a word list, not a parser; a wrong binding costs an extra marker
in the annotation.
@item
@emph{Nothing pointed at}: ``this'' and ``here'' fall back to point in
the selected window with confidence 0.3
(@code{cmacs-deixis-fallback-to-point}).  ``It'' never falls back.
@end itemize

The vocabulary is @code{cmacs-deixis-words}, an alist of word to role
(@code{object}, @code{place}, @code{pronoun}); replace it for another
language.

@node Deixis Word Times
@section Word times

@code{cmacs-deixis-result-words} returns @code{((WORD START-MS END-MS
. FLAGS) ...)}, times counted from the start of the audio the recognizer
was given.  In order of preference:

@enumerate
@item
The result's own @code{:words}.  New: sherpa-onnx offline (@code{asr})
and streaming (@code{stream}) results carry an optional @code{:words}
entry, a list of @code{(WORD START-MS END-MS)}, whenever the model gives
token timestamps.  It is built in C (@code{build_words} in
@file{cmacs/sherpa/cmacs-sherpa-asr.c}) from the token array that used
to be flattened into one segment: subword pieces are merged into words
(a piece starting with SentencePiece's marker or a space starts a word;
punctuation stays on the word it follows; a CJK character is a word by
itself), and a word ends where the next begins and no later than its
last piece's start plus the piece's duration (300 ms when the model
gives none).  Existing keys are unchanged, and a model without
timestamps simply has no @code{:words}.  For a stream the times are on
the stream's axis, the one @code{:start} and @code{:end} are on, and
@code{cmacs-speech}'s stream @code{on-final} alist passes @code{:words}
through.
@item
@code{:tokens} with @code{:timestamps}, merged in Lisp by
@code{cmacs-deixis-merge-tokens} (the same rules).
@item
@code{:segments} (whisper): each segment's words are spread across the
segment's span in proportion to their length and flagged @code{:approx}
(@code{cmacs-deixis-interpolate-words}).  Nothing changed in
@file{cmacs/whisper/}.
@item
Only @code{:text}: @code{cmacs-deixis-fallback-word-ms} (350) per word,
@code{:approx}.
@end enumerate

The text the loop sends has the wake phrase cut off the front, so its
tokens are matched to the recognizer's words from the end; a token spelt
differently (``21'' against ``twenty one'') takes a time interpolated
from its neighbours.

@node Deixis Clock
@section The clock, and how wrong it can be

A word's absolute time is the segment start, plus the word's offset,
plus @code{cmacs-deixis-latency-ms}.  The segment start is taken when
the voice loop opens a speech segment:
@code{cmacs-voice-loop-segment-start-functions} runs with the segment id
and the audio the segment already holds (pre-roll plus the onset
frames), and deixis records now less that lead.  ``Now'' is
@code{cmacs-deixis-now-ms}: @code{gowl-pointer-history-now}
(@code{CLOCK_MONOTONIC} milliseconds, the clock the compositor's ring
stamps samples with) when the compositor is built in, else wall time,
which is then only ever compared with samples stamped by the same
function.

@strong{None of the following has been measured.}  It was not possible
to run audio while this was written; the figures are reasoning about the
code, and the first must be calibrated on real hardware.

@table @asis
@item Capture latency (microphone, PipeWire/GStreamer buffers, delivery in chunks)
Tens of ms to about 100 ms, device dependent; words look later than they
were.  Corrected by @code{cmacs-deixis-latency-ms} (default @minus{}60,
an assumption).
@item Frame quantisation of the segment start
Up to one 32 ms frame, either way.  Absorbed by the window.
@item Pre-roll accounting
Should be exact: the frames are counted.
@item Emacs busy when the frame arrives (GC, redisplay)
Usually a few ms, occasionally over 100; words look later.  Not
correctable.
@item Token timestamps (sherpa)
One encoder frame, 40--80 ms; a token's time is when it was emitted, and
streaming models emit late.
@item Interpolated word times (whisper)
Hundreds of ms inside a long segment, either way.  Flagged
@code{:approx}; use a token-timestamp model.
@item Pointer sampling
20 ms (gowl), 100 ms (pointers sampled from Lisp).
@item The speaker
People point 100--300 ms before or after the word.  This is what the
window and the dwell are for.
@end table

With a whisper model the binding is only as good as the interpolation:
fine for a short ``close that'', unreliable for two demonstratives close
together in a long sentence.  A sherpa token-timestamp model is the
right engine for this.  The streaming recognizer's assumption that token
times count from the segment start was read from sherpa-onnx's sources,
not observed.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-deixis-calibrate}: say ``now'' to the voice loop and
press a key at the same instant.  When the transcript arrives it reports
the difference between the key's time and the word's, and the
@code{cmacs-deixis-latency-ms} that would make it zero.  Your reaction
time is in that number, so do it a few times and take the middle.

@node Deixis Pointers
@section Pointers

``Motion never reaches Lisp'': the gowl pointer is sampled in C.
@file{cmacs/gowl/cmacs-gowl-pointer-history.c} keeps a fixed ring of 512
samples --- monotonic ms, layout x and y, the id of the client under the
pointer (0: none), the monitor index --- filled by a 20 ms timer on
gowl's own @code{wl_event_loop} (never GLib's default context, which
under cmacs is Emacs's thread) and written under the gowl lock.  The
timer exists only while the history is enabled.  A tick stores a sample
only when something changed, so an entry means ``from this time on'';
the ring covers about ten seconds of a pointer that never rests and far
longer otherwise.  There is no allocation per sample, and nothing is
sampled while the session is locked.

@defun gowl-pointer-history-enable enable
Start or stop sampling.  Returns non-@code{nil} when sampling is now on,
@code{nil} with no compositor.  Turning it off empties the ring.
@end defun

@defun gowl-pointer-history-enabled-p
Whether the history is sampling.
@end defun

@defun gowl-pointer-history-at ms
A plist @code{(:time :x :y :client :client-id :monitor)} for the latest
sample at or before @var{ms}; @code{nil} outside the ring.
@end defun

@defun gowl-pointer-history-dwell from-ms to-ms &optional radius
The same plus @code{:duration}, for the run of samples (same window,
within @var{radius} pixels, default 48) that lasted longest in the
window; x and y are its time-weighted mean.
@end defun

@defun gowl-pointer-history-now
The monotonic clock in milliseconds.
@end defun

Clients are stored by id, never by pointer.  A lookup turns an id back
into an object only after finding it in the live client list, so
@code{:client} is @code{nil} for a window that has closed and
@code{:client-id} still says which it was.  The resolver turns that into
a @code{:stale} referent: no command acts on it, and the legend reads
``a window that has since closed (client 12)''.

Every DEFUN that touches the ring takes @code{cmacs_gowl_lock_scoped ()}
and returns through @code{unbind_to}.  @code{gowl-stop} must call
@code{cmacs_gowl_pointer_history_forget ()} from
@code{cmacs_gowl_forget_compositor_state} (the timer is a source on the
compositor's loop); as a second line of defence the file remembers which
compositor owns its timer and never removes a source from another one's
loop.

When the window under the pointer is Emacs's own frame, the referent is
refined to the Emacs window and buffer position there with
@code{posn-at-x-y}.  That lookup happens when the transcript arrives,
against the windows as they are then, and assumes the frame's pixel
coordinates are the layout position minus the client's origin (true for
an undecorated frame at output scale 1; unverified at other scales).
Such referents are flagged @code{:approximate}.

The other pointers:

@table @code
@item emacs
@code{mouse-pixel-position}, sampled by a timer every
@code{cmacs-deixis-sample-interval} (0.1 s), only where no compositor is
running.
@item vr
@code{cmacs-lrg-vr-pointed-window}, sampled by the same timer while a VR
session is active.  Window granularity: the laser's accessor gives the
window, so the position is that window's point.
@item hand
A camera-tracked hand, in two cases: a bare hand holding the pointing
pose (a ring of its own, below), and with
@code{cmacs-handtrack-pointer-mode} the hand that @emph{is} the gowl
pointer (the ring above already has it).
@end table

@subheading A bare hand

Without @code{cmacs-handtrack-pointer-mode} --- or for the hand that is
not driving the pointer --- you point by holding the pointing pose in
front of the camera: index finger out, the other three curled.  Say
``that'' while the fingertip rests over a window.

@emph{What this is and is not.}  One camera sees where a fingertip is in
its image; it cannot see where the finger is aimed.  So a bare hand is a
cursor held in the air, not a ray: the index fingertip is mapped onto
the screen through the same active region and output the hand cursor
uses (@code{cmacs-handtrack-pointer-region},
@code{cmacs-handtrack-pointer-output}), by the same C function.  A
fingertip at the region's edge is at the screen's edge; one more than a
tenth of the region beyond it points at nothing.  With
@code{cmacs-handtrack-pointer-output} @code{nil} the output is the one
the mouse pointer is on, so a bare hand cannot reach a second monitor
unless that option names it.  A finger aimed straight at the camera is
foreshortened to a stub and does not read as pointing; hold it up, as
you would to touch a pane of glass.  Every hand referent is flagged
@code{:approximate} and its confidence is capped
(@code{cmacs-deixis-hand-confidence}, 0.9).

@emph{The pose} (@code{cmacs_ht_point_pose} in
@file{cmacs/handtrack/cmacs-handtrack-point-history.c}, pure: 26 joints
in, a verdict out).  For each finger the classifier takes its
@dfn{reach}: how much further from the wrist the fingertip is than its
own knuckle, in metres on the hand scaled to the standard palm
(0.094 m), which is what makes the pinch thresholds distance-independent
too.  A straight finger reaches about 0.075; one folded into the palm
about zero or less.  Pointing is index reach of at least
@code{cmacs-handtrack-point-extend} (0.055) with none of middle, ring
and little past @code{cmacs-handtrack-point-curl} (0.035), and the thumb
not on the index tip (that is a pinch, which means something else).  The
thumb is otherwise ignored.  Confidence is 0.5 exactly on a threshold
and 1.0 from 0.02 m clear of both.

@emph{The ring.}  512 fixed entries: monotonic ms, hand, pose
confidence, the position as fractions across the output and as layout
pixels, the id of the client under it, the monitor index.  One entry per
camera frame in which a hand was pointing and none otherwise --- unlike
the mouse ring an entry is an event, not a state --- so about seventeen
seconds of uninterrupted pointing.  It is appended on the main thread,
where inference results arrive (@code{ht_on_result}), from C structs
only: no allocation, no Lisp object, nothing that can signal.  The
position is One-Euro smoothed (@code{cmacs-handtrack-point-min-cutoff},
@code{-beta}), restarting whenever the hand leaves the pose so it does
not glide in from where it last was.  What is under the fingertip is the
compositor's own hit test, asked through
@code{cmacs_gowl_pointer_history_hit}, which takes the gowl lock itself;
on a locked session it refuses and nothing is stored.  With no
compositor only the fractions mean anything, and they are resolved
against Emacs's frames when the word arrives.

@emph{The clock} is the mouse ring's, CLOCK_MONOTONIC milliseconds.  A
sample carries the time its camera frame @emph{arrived} (taken on the
camera's streaming thread, before inference), not the time its result
reached the main thread, so the tens of milliseconds the networks take
are not in it.  What is left is the camera's own delay --- exposure,
readout, transport --- which the pipeline does not report:
@code{cmacs-handtrack-point-latency-ms} (40, an assumption) is taken off
every sample, and @kbd{M-x cmacs-handtrack-point-calibrate} measures it
(pinch with one hand, press a key with the other at the same instant;
the frame the pinch closed in is stamped the way pointing samples are).
In a build with hand tracking and no compositor
@code{cmacs-deixis-now-ms} is wall time; the provider measures the
constant between the two clocks and applies it.

@emph{A rest is required.}  @code{cmacs-handtrack-point-history-dwell}
groups one hand's samples into rests --- over the same window, within
@code{cmacs-handtrack-point-radius} (96 px) of where the run began, with
no gap over 250 ms --- and answers with the one that lasted longest
inside the word's window, but only if it lasted
@code{cmacs-handtrack-point-dwell-ms} (120) there.  A hand passing
through the pose on its way somewhere else refers to nothing, and the
provider does not fall back to wherever the hand was at the word's
instant.

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-enable enable
Start or stop noting where a bare hand points.  Returns non-@code{nil}
when it is now on.  Turning it off empties the ring.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-at ms
@code{(:time :x :y :client-id :monitor :hand :confidence :u :v :layout)}
for the latest sample at or before @var{ms}; @code{nil} if there is none
within 250 ms.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-dwell from-ms to-ms &optional radius min-ms
The same, as means over the rest, plus @code{:duration} and
@code{:since} (when the rest really began, possibly long before
@var{from-ms}).
@end defun

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-clear
Empty the ring.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-status
@code{:enabled :samples :capacity :stored :pointing :pinch-left
:pinch-right :latency-ms :now}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-handtrack-point-history-now
The monotonic clock in ms.
@end defun

The plists have the mouse ring's keys so the resolver treats them alike,
with one difference: there is no @code{:client}, only
@code{:client-id}, and the provider looks the id up among the live
clients (@code{gowl-list-clients}).  From there a hand sample goes
through @code{cmacs-deixis--gowl-referent} exactly as a mouse sample
does --- a desktop window, refined to the Emacs window and buffer
position inside Emacs's own frame, a @code{:stale} referent for a window
that has closed, else the monitor.

The ring is on only between the provider's @code{:start} and
@code{:stop} (while deixis is sampling), and it is emptied when it is
turned off @emph{and} whenever hand tracking closes: where you pointed
does not outlive the session that saw it.

There is no on-screen marker of where a bare hand is taken to be
pointing.  Nothing existing draws one cheaply --- the hand cursor is the
real pointer, the VR laser lives in the 3D scene --- so the feedback is
after the fact: the echo area names what each hand-pointed word resolved
to before a command acts on it (@code{cmacs-deixis-announce-hand}).

@subheading Which pointer is believed

Every registered pointer is asked, and
@code{cmacs-deixis-choose-referent} picks among the answers.  The two
kinds fail in opposite ways: a mouse always has an answer, because it is
always somewhere; a hand has one only while it holds the pose.  ``First
answer wins'' would let whichever is listed first shadow the other for
good.  So a referent may say when its pointer came to rest on it
(@code{:since}), and one that came to rest more than
@code{cmacs-deixis-parked-ms} (2000) before the word's window opened is
@dfn{parked} --- where it was left, not where anyone is pointing.

@itemize
@item
The first candidate, in @code{cmacs-deixis-pointer-order}, that is not
parked wins.
@item
When every candidate is parked, the first.
@item
A pointer that gives no @code{:since} is taken at its word.
@end itemize

A mouse left lying where it was does not outvote a hand raised to point,
whichever is listed first; a hand left lying in the pointing pose does
not outvote a mouse just moved; when both are in use the order decides.
For the gowl pointer @code{:since} is the time of the ring entry in
force when its dwell began (a mouse nobody is touching makes no new
entries); for the pointers sampled from Lisp, the ring entry's own time;
for a hand, the first sample of the rest.  The function is pure ---
referents in, one of them out.

@defun cmacs-deixis-choose-referent candidates from
Choose among @var{candidates}, the referents of the pointers that had an
answer in order of preference, for a word whose window opens at
@var{from} (ms).
@end defun

@defun cmacs-deixis-register-pointer name function &rest props
Register pointing device @var{name}.  @var{function} is called with a
time in ms and returns a referent plist or @code{nil};
@code{cmacs-deixis-lookup-window} is bound to @code{(FROM-MS . TO-MS)}
around the word.  @var{props} may give @code{:start} and @code{:stop},
called when sampling begins and ends.  A pointer must hold positions
only between the two, and only in memory.  One that signals costs its
own answer, not the utterance.
@end defun

@lisp
(cmacs-deixis-register-pointer
 'gaze
 (lambda (ms)
   (when-let* ((hit (my-gaze-history-at ms)))
     (list :kind 'buffer-position :window (car hit)
           :buffer (window-buffer (car hit))
           :position (cdr hit) :confidence 0.7
           :label (format "buffer: %s"
                          (buffer-name (window-buffer (car hit)))))))
 :start #'my-gaze-history-start
 :stop #'my-gaze-history-stop)
@end lisp

@node Deixis Commands
@section Commands

Simple utterances run locally.  An utterance is reduced to a canonical
form --- lower-cased, @code{cmacs-deixis-filler-words} dropped, each
bound demonstrative replaced by @samp{<object>} or @samp{<place>} ---
and matched against each command's regexp: ``Please move that window
over there'' is @samp{move <object> <place>}.  A command runs only when
every placeholder has a referent that still exists; otherwise the
utterance goes on to the route, annotated, so a model can ask what was
meant.

@table @code
@item move
``move / put / send / bring / take that there''.  A desktop window: to
the pointed tag; onto another window of the same screen (a swap, the
only ``there'' a tiling layout has); else to the pointed monitor, and a
floating window also takes the pointed half.  An Emacs buffer: into the
pointed Emacs window.
@item close
``close / quit / delete / kill that''.  Closes the desktop window, or
deletes the Emacs window (``kill'' kills the buffer).  Confirmed first.
@item focus
``focus / go / switch / select / jump there''.  Focuses the desktop
window, or selects the Emacs window and goes to the position.
@item swap
``swap / exchange this and that''.  Two desktop windows
(@code{gowl-swap-clients}) or two Emacs windows
(@code{window-swap-states}).
@item open
``open / show / visit that here''.  The file or URL at the pointed
buffer position, in the pointed Emacs window.
@end table

They use existing DEFUNs only (@code{gowl-move-client-to-monitor},
@code{gowl-client-set-geometry}, @code{gowl-set-tags},
@code{gowl-swap-clients}, @code{gowl-close-client},
@code{gowl-focus-client}).  A window embedded in an Emacs window is not
moved.  @code{open} reads the one file name or URL at the position,
locally, to act on it; that is the only place buffer text is read, and
it is not sent anywhere.

Destructive commands ask first
(@code{cmacs-deixis-confirm-destructive}): through
@code{cmacs-voice-confirm} --- a spoken yes/no or y/n, with the loop
armed so the answer needs no wake phrase --- when the voice commands are
loaded, else @code{y-or-n-p}.  The question names what would be closed,
whatever pointed at it, and ends ``(pointed at by hand)'' when a bare
hand did.

A bare hand's aim is approximate, so any command acting on a hand
referent first says in the echo area what each word was taken to mean
--- ``Deixis (by hand): that = window: librewolf --- 'wlroots docs'
(client 12)'' (@code{cmacs-deixis-announce-hand}).  Labels only, as in
the legend.

@defmac cmacs-deixis-define-command name pattern arglist body@dots{}
Define deictic command @var{name}.  @var{arglist} names the referents of
the placeholders in spoken order.  @var{body} may start with a
docstring, then @code{:confirm @var{form}} (non-@code{nil}: confirm
first; a string is the question).  It reports a refusal with
@code{user-error}, and can tell its verbs apart through
@code{cmacs-deixis-command-words}.  Commands run from the command loop.
@end defmac

@lisp
(cmacs-deixis-define-command pin
    "\\`\\(?:pin\\|keep\\) <object>\\'" (that)
  "\"Pin that\": make the pointed window sticky."
  (unless (plist-get that :client)
    (user-error "That is not a desktop window"))
  (gowl-set-client-sticky (plist-get that :client) t))
@end lisp

@node Deixis Privacy
@section Privacy

@itemize
@item
The pointers are sampled only while @code{cmacs-deixis-mode} is on
@emph{and} the voice loop is listening (its state is neither
@code{idle} nor @code{paused}).
@item
Samples live in fixed rings in memory: the compositor's 512 entries, the
hand's 512, and at most 128 for each pointer sampled from Lisp.
Stopping empties them.
@item
The hand's ring is also emptied whenever hand tracking closes
(@code{cmacs-handtrack-close}, so turning @code{cmacs-handtrack-mode}
off or losing the camera), and holds nothing for the hand driving the
pointer.  No camera frame and no landmark is kept: a sample is a time, a
hand, a confidence, a position and a client id.
@item
Nothing is written to disk and nothing is sampled on a locked session.
@item
What reaches a model is the legend: ids, titles, buffer names and
positions.  Never buffer text.
@end itemize

@node Deixis Customization
@section Customization

@table @code
@item cmacs-deixis-latency-ms
Added to a word's time before lookup (default @minus{}60); calibrate it.
@item cmacs-deixis-window-before-ms
@itemx cmacs-deixis-window-after-ms
The window around a word (150, 300).
@item cmacs-deixis-words
Word to role.
@item cmacs-deixis-clause-followers
After these, ``that'' is a conjunction.
@item cmacs-deixis-pronoun-blockers
After these, ``it'' points at nothing.
@item cmacs-deixis-filler-words
Dropped before command matching.
@item cmacs-deixis-pointer-order
Which pointer is preferred among those not parked
(@code{(vr hand gowl emacs)}).
@item cmacs-deixis-parked-ms
A pointer at rest longer than this before the word gives way (2000).
@item cmacs-deixis-hand-confidence
The most a bare hand's referent can be trusted (0.9).
@item cmacs-deixis-announce-hand
Say what a hand-pointed word resolved to before acting (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-deixis-elsewhere-confidence
Pointer confidence that overrides an active region (0.8).
@item cmacs-deixis-fallback-to-point
``This'' with nothing pointed at means point.
@item cmacs-deixis-fallback-word-ms
Per-word guess when a transcript has no times (350).
@item cmacs-deixis-sample-interval
Period of the pointers sampled from Lisp (0.1 s).
@item cmacs-deixis-confirm-destructive
Confirm ``close that''.
@item cmacs-deixis-annotate-for-commands
Annotate on the @code{commands} route too.
@end table

The hook @code{cmacs-deixis-resolved-functions} runs with @code{(TEXT
BINDINGS)} after every resolved utterance.

The bare hand's own options are in @file{cmacs-handtrack.el} (group
@code{cmacs-handtrack-point}) and are pushed to C through
@code{cmacs-handtrack-set-option}:

@table @code
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-hand
Which hand's pointing is noted (@code{any}).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-extend
Index reach that is ``straight'', metres on the standard palm (0.055).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-curl
The most the other three fingers may reach (0.035).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-min-confidence
Pose confidence a frame needs to be noted (0.5).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-dwell-ms
How long a hand must rest to be a referent (120).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-radius
Layout pixels within which it is one rest (96).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-latency-ms
The camera's delay, taken off each sample's time (40); calibrate it.
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-min-cutoff
One-Euro minimum cutoff for the fingertip, Hz (1.2).
@item cmacs-handtrack-point-beta
One-Euro speed coefficient (0.02).
@end table

The voice loop gained three additive hooks, usable by anything:
@code{cmacs-voice-loop-segment-start-functions} (@code{(SEG LEAD-MS)}
when a speech segment opens), @code{cmacs-voice-loop-utterance-filter-function}
(@code{(TEXT SEG RESULT)} before the route; returns the text to send, or
@code{nil} when it took the utterance; a filter that signals does not
eat the utterance) and @code{cmacs-voice-loop--final-result}.

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-deixis-tests.el} is pure ERT with stub pointers
and synthetic word times.  It also covers the bare hand with the
history's DEFUNs stubbed: the arbitration table
(@code{cmacs-deixis-choose-referent}), a still mouse against a raised
hand and a lying hand against a moved mouse in both orders, resolution
through the mouse's path (window, stale window, monitor) and against
frames, the dwell requirement, pointer mode, ``move that there'' from
two rests, the confirmation and announcement, the clock offset, and that
the ring follows sampling.

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-handtrack-point-tests.el} drives the C: hands are
built as MediaPipe landmarks (a flat hand with each finger straight or
folded) and run through @code{cmacs-handtrack--simulate-point}, the path
live results take.  It covers the pose table (pointing against open
palm, fist, two fingers, pinch; both hands; half the size), the
thresholds as options, the mapping through the region onto outputs at
and away from the origin, off-screen, read-by-time across two rests, the
dwell requirement and a sweeping hand, one hand against the other,
pinch-onset stamping and the calibration arithmetic, that closing the
tracker or turning the history off empties the ring, and ``move that
there'' end to end from landmarks and word times.  Source guards, which
need no C, check that the ring's file sees neither @file{lisp.h} nor
gowl, allocates nothing and writes nothing, that results reach it from
@code{ht_on_result} stamped with the frame's time, and that the cursor
and the pointing hand share one mapping function.

Not covered, and needing a live session: the C ring against a real
compositor, the C word builder against a real sherpa model, the pointing
pose against a real hand and camera (the thresholds are from hand
proportions, not from recordings), and every timing figure above.
